Executive Summary

Speech and language technology is increasingly central to every aspect of our lives – how we work, socialise, learn, access information, pursue our leisure activities etc. The digital revolution is English-language dominated and is described as a ‘digital timebomb’ [1] for lesser-used languages, accelerating the catastrophic rate at which languages are being lost. Language transmission depends on the younger, tech-savvy generation, and technology provision for one’s own language will undoubtedly be a critical factor in its survival. On the positive side, speech and language technology development, underpinned by linguistic research, offers unprecedented opportunities for language maintenance and revitalisation. The Digital Plan is therefore one important strand in the Government’s plan for the Irish language.
This document outlines the research and development needed to bring Irish into the digital age. It will require an interdisciplinary effort, involving linguists, engineers, computational linguists, speech scientists, and software specialists. It is important that this work is carried out in close collaboration with the Irish language community, and with stakeholders in areas such as education, disability and access.
The specific areas of application which will be most important for Irish, will often differ from those prioritised for languages like English, where development is primarily driven by commercial considerations. Priorities for Irish need to answer to the most urgent needs of the Irish language community and to the goals of language maintenance. It is thus a guiding principle of the Digital Plan that the challenges addressed, and solutions adopted should be tailored to the Irish context, and founded on a deep knowledge of the language.
The research and development envisaged in the Digital Plan involves three strands of activity, presented in the three sections of this document. These are I Resources, II Core Technologies and III Applications. These three strands cover the range from basic to applied research and need to be progressed in parallel.

