Semantically annotated corpora are corpora that contain semantic annotations regarding individual lexical items and grammatical structures illustrated in Figure 3.4. A corpus in which arguments of verbs and other predicates are annotated is known as a proposition bank, e.g. PropBank [1] (see Chapter 2, semantics). All verbs in a selected corpus are annotated, which gives wide coverage of verb usage and variability. Named entities can also be labelled which helps to distinguish types of noun phrases and determine their semantic roles. This information is necessary for downstream tasks such as quality information retrieval, text summarisation and machine translation.

